---
import { defaultLocale } from "@skopli/ui/i18n";
import { posthogHost } from "@skopli/ui/analytics";
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { href, t as translate } from "../lib/i18n.ts";
import { marketingUrl } from "../site.ts";

const t = translate(defaultLocale);
---

<DocsLayout
  locale={defaultLocale}
  slug="privacy"
  title={t("privacy")}
  description="What this site records about a visit, and what it does not."
>
  <p>
    Skopli is a library. It has no accounts, no sign-in, and no telemetry in the SDK. This notice
    covers the docs site and <a href={marketingUrl}>skopli.com</a> only.
  </p>
  <h2 id="what-is-recorded">What is recorded</h2>
  <p>
    Both sites send anonymous usage events to PostHog at <code>{posthogHost}</code>, hosted in
    Frankfurt: one page view per page load, plus a named event when you copy a page as Markdown or
    open it in an AI assistant. The events exist to show which pages are read and which handoffs are
    used, so the docs can be improved.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each event carries what the stock PostHog browser library attaches: the page URL and path, the
    referrer, the browser, operating system, and device type, screen and viewport size, language,
    timezone, and the library version. Like any web server, PostHog receives the IP address of the
    request. Skopli does not use it.
  </p>
  <p>
    PostHog keeps events for the project's plan retention period, which PostHog sets at one year on
    its free plan and seven years on paid plans. Nothing is exported elsewhere.
  </p>
  <h2 id="what-is-not-recorded">What is not recorded</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No cookies and no analytics identifiers: PostHog runs with in-memory persistence, so nothing survives the tab.</li>
    <li>No person profiles, so events are not linked across visits.</li>
    <li>No session recording, no surveys, no automatic click capture.</li>
    <li>Nothing on localhost or other local hosts, so development runs send no events.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2 id="stored-in-your-browser">Stored in your browser</h2>
  <p>
    Your theme choice and the last selected language tab are kept in <code>localStorage</code> so the
    site remembers them. They never leave your browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href={href("/")}>Back to the docs</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
